===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} A [[tale#Noun|tale]] or [[story#Noun|story]] which is [[fantastic#Adjective|fantastic]] and [[greatly]] [[exaggerated#Adjective|exaggerated]]; also, an [[account#Noun|account]] of [[questionable]] [[veracity]]; a [[lie#Noun|lie]], an [[untruth]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Banbury story of a cock and a bull|cock-and-bull story|fish story|tall story|traveller's tale}}
#: {{ux|en|He returned on Monday with a '''tall tale''' about a 100-pound fish he had caught.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work={{w|National Geographic Magazine}}|date=August 4, 2020|author=Richard Conniff|url=https://www.nationalgeographic.com/magazine/2020/09/they-may-look-goofy-but-ostriches-are-nobodys-fool-feature/|title=They may look goofy, but ostriches are nobody’s fool|passage=The head-in-sand idea is a threadbare, 2,000-year-old hand-me-down from the Roman naturalist Pliny, who sometimes passed on '''tall tales'''.}}

